# Thiago Castro Skills

A public collection of reusable AI skills for learning, thinking, writing, research, and productivity.

Each skill is designed as an independent capability that can be used to guide AI tools through a specific method, framework, or workflow.

These are not general-purpose agents. Each skill focuses on solving one clearly defined problem.

## Available Skills

| Skill | Category | Description |
|---|---|---|
| [Feynman Technique](skills/feynman-technique) | Learning | Helps users understand complex subjects by explaining them simply, identifying knowledge gaps, and improving their explanation. |

More skills will be added over time.

## Repository Structure

```text
thiago_castro-skills/
├── README.md
├── LICENSE
│
└── skills/
    └── skill-name/
        ├── SKILL.md
        ├── README.md
        ├── examples/
        ├── references/
        └── evals/
```

Each skill may contain:

- `SKILL.md`: the core instructions followed by the AI.
- `README.md`: human-readable documentation about the skill.
- `examples/`: practical examples of inputs and outputs.
- `references/`: supporting knowledge or documentation.
- `evals/`: test cases used to evaluate the skill.

Only `SKILL.md` is required. The other files and folders are added when they provide additional value.

## How to Use a Skill

1. Open the folder of the skill you want to use.
2. Copy the complete skill folder.
3. Add it to the skills directory supported by your AI tool.
4. Ask the AI to perform a task related to the skill.

Depending on the AI tool, you may also reference the `SKILL.md` file directly in your project instructions.

### Example

To use the Feynman Technique skill:

```text
skills/feynman-technique/
```

Example request:

```text
Help me understand compound interest using the Feynman Technique.
```

## Design Principles

Every skill in this repository should:

- Solve one clearly defined problem.
- Work independently from other skills.
- Explain when it should and should not be used.
- Define the required inputs.
- Provide a repeatable workflow.
- Define the expected output.
- Avoid unnecessary complexity.
- Avoid private, confidential, or client-specific information.
- Include examples and evaluation cases when useful.

## Skill Naming Convention

Skill folders should use lowercase letters and hyphens:

```text
feynman-technique
five-whys
socratic-questioning
first-principles-thinking
decision-matrix
```

Avoid spaces, underscores, capital letters, or version numbers in folder names.

## Creating a New Skill

Create a new folder inside `skills/`:

```text
skills/new-skill-name/
```

The minimum structure is:

```text
skills/new-skill-name/
└── SKILL.md
```

A more complete structure is:

```text
skills/new-skill-name/
├── SKILL.md
├── README.md
├── examples/
├── references/
└── evals/
```

## Contributing

Suggestions, corrections, improvements, and new skill ideas are welcome.

When contributing a new skill:

1. Keep the skill focused on one capability.
2. Use a clear and descriptive folder name.
3. Include a valid `SKILL.md`.
4. Do not include confidential or copyrighted material without permission.
5. Add examples or test cases whenever possible.

You can contribute by opening an issue or submitting a pull request.

